# The OFX conversion's own columns, in the order `convert::ofx` emits them.
skip 1
fields date,amount,name,memo,trntype,fitid,checknum
date-format %Y-%m-%d
description %name
account1 assets:bank:checking
account2 expenses:unknown

# The one line this whole feature turns on. `comment` is an ordinary top-level
# hledger field, so this writes `; id:FIT0001` onto the transaction and hledger
# reads it back as the tag `id` — no new grammar, and a rules file anyone can
# run from a terminal. Ledgeline matches a re-download against the journal on it.
comment id:%fitid

# OFX's own way of saying "authorized, not yet posted" is TRNTYPE HOLD, and it
# is the only status signal an OFX statement carries — there is no status column
# for `convert::ofx` to emit. Mapping it here is what makes a settled hold a
# STATUS-ONLY difference rather than an invisible one.
if %trntype HOLD
  status !
if %trntype DEBIT
  status *
if %trntype CREDIT
  status *
